<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      Row-level security for audit_events — the last tenant-scoped table in the product that had
      none. Measured live against audit_db before this changeset:

          relname                | rls | forced | policies
          audit_events           |  f  |   f    |    0
          audit_events_2026_01   |  f  |   f    |    0     (and all 13 other partitions)

      Every other tenant-scoped service FORCEs RLS on its tenant tables. The audit log — which
      holds every login, void, refund, role change and password reset for every tenant — did not.

      Tenant isolation here was defended at exactly one layer: AuditQueryController takes tenantId
      from the verified JWT and declares no tenant parameter, so no caller can influence it. That
      is a genuinely strong structural control and it is why this was a gap rather than a live
      leak. It is also the only one, and it does not extend to AuditInternalController (which takes
      a tenant as a query parameter, gated on a shared secret) nor to anything that reaches the
      table outside this service.

      ── The partitioning problem, measured rather than assumed ──────────────────────────────────

      audit_events is PARTITION BY RANGE (occurred_at). The obvious implementation — enable and
      FORCE on the parent, one policy on the parent — is WRONG, and silently so. Probed on this
      project's actual PostgreSQL 18.4:

        * A policy on the parent does not appear on the partition's catalog row at all:
          parent relrowsecurity=t/relforcerowsecurity=t, partition f/f, zero rows in pg_policies
          for the partition.
        * It is applied only to queries that name the PARENT. A query naming the PARTITION
          directly gets no policy whatsoever. With the parent fully protected, a NOSUPERUSER role
          that owns the tables ran `SELECT count(*) FROM ev_p1` and received BOTH tenants' rows —
          2 of 2 — while `SELECT count(*) FROM ev` correctly returned 1.
        * A partition created AFTER the parent is enabled and forced inherits nothing:
          relrowsecurity=f, relforcerowsecurity=f, zero policies. So a parent-only control would
          appear to work for exactly as long as nobody created next month's partition.

      So the control has to be applied to the parent AND to every partition individually, AND to
      every partition created in the future. All three are below. The parent policy is not
      redundant: it is what covers queries through audit_events itself, which is every query the
      application actually issues.

      One consequence worth stating because it is load-bearing for this table: because each
      partition carries its own ENABLE + FORCE + policy, a partition stays protected after
      AuditArchivalService DETACHes it. That service detaches rather than drops, keeping up to 84
      months of history as standalone tables; had the control lived only on the parent, every
      detach would have produced an unprotected standalone table holding seven-year-old audit
      records for every tenant. Verified: after DETACH the partition kept rls=t, forced=t and its
      policy, and the owning NOSUPERUSER role saw 0 foreign rows and its own 2.

      ── READ THIS BEFORE WRITING ANY FUTURE MIGRATION ON audit_events ───────────────────────────

      From this changeset onward, a data step and a DDL step in the same migration SEE DIFFERENT
      DATABASES.

      Liquibase runs as the table OWNER on a connection with no app.current_tenant_id set. Under
      FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY the owner is subject to the policy like anyone else, so a SELECT or
      UPDATE issued by a migration matches ZERO ROWS — silently, with no error and no warning.
      ALTER TABLE is not filtered by RLS and still applies to every row. So:

          UPDATE audit_events SET x = ... ;             (touches nothing, reports success)
          ALTER TABLE audit_events ADD CONSTRAINT ...;  (applies to rows the UPDATE never saw)

      That is not hypothetical in this repository. pos-service's V22__order_discount_reason.sql
      records the wreck in its own header: the repair silently no-oped, the constraint then
      rejected the very rows it was meant to have fixed, and the service would not boot
      (SQL State 23502, "contains null values").

      What to do instead:

        * Order matters absolutely. Any backfill or normalisation must run BEFORE the statement
          that enables FORCE — not after it, and not in a later changeset.
        * Need a constraint over existing rows? Add it NOT VALID. New writes are checked by every
          path, pre-existing rows are neither checked nor silently restated, and it cannot fail on
          creation whatever an unseen customer database holds. Then finish it OPERATIONALLY: set
          the tenant GUC per tenant on a credentialed connection, repair, and VALIDATE CONSTRAINT
          (which takes only SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE and is safe against a live system).
        * Do NOT wrap the data step in DISABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY or NO FORCE. Suppressing a
          protection to get work done is the antipattern that already produced one security
          finding in this codebase, and on THIS table it would also mean a migration could rewrite
          audit history that the append-only trigger exists to make unrewritable.

      This changeset itself contains no data steps at all — only DDL, grants and a function
      replacement — which is why it is safe in the presence of the rule it is documenting.

      ── Why this cannot break ingestion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

      An audit pipeline that silently stops recording is worse than one with a missing policy, and
      a WITH CHECK policy refuses every insert made without app.current_tenant_id set. The write
      path was traced before this was written: AllEventsConsumer delegates to
      TenantAwareMessageProcessor.process(), which is @Transactional and calls
      TenantGucHelper.apply() — a transaction-local set_config on the connection already bound to
      the open transaction — BEFORE the handler runs any DML. AuditIngestionService.ingest() joins
      that same transaction (propagation REQUIRED), so its persist() runs on the same connection
      that carries the GUC. The GUC value is envelope.tenantId(), which is also the value written
      to the tenant_id column, so the WITH CHECK predicate compares a value against itself.
    -->

    <changeSet id="audit-1.0.0-030a-audit-events-rls-parent" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <sql>ALTER TABLE audit_events ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;</sql>

        <!--
          FORCE, not merely ENABLE. PostgreSQL exempts a table's OWNER from its own policies
          unless FORCE is set, so ENABLE alone yields a policy that is present in the catalog,
          visible in pg_policies, and completely inert for the role that owns the table. That
          exact defect shipped 33 inert tables across 16 tenants in this product and no test
          caught it. It matters here specifically because create_audit_partition() below runs
          SECURITY DEFINER and therefore creates partitions owned by the same role Liquibase
          runs as — and because the test harness's own role owns these tables outright.
        -->
        <sql>ALTER TABLE audit_events FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;</sql>

        <!--
          The predicate is the one every other service in this product uses, character for
          character, so that a reader who knows one knows all of them:

              tenant_id = (NULLIF(current_setting('app.current_tenant_id', true), ''))::uuid

          The two details that make it safe. current_setting(..., true) returns NULL instead of
          raising when the GUC was never set on this session. NULLIF(...,'') maps the empty
          string to NULL as well, which matters because TenantAwareDataSource writes an EMPTY
          value — not no value — on every checkout made without a tenant, precisely so that a
          pooled connection can never inherit the previous borrower's tenant. Both cases produce
          `tenant_id = NULL`, which is NULL, which is not true: no rows readable and no rows
          insertable. The policy fails CLOSED.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON audit_events
                USING (tenant_id = (NULLIF(current_setting('app.current_tenant_id', true), ''))::uuid);
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP POLICY IF EXISTS tenant_isolation ON audit_events;</sql>
            <sql>ALTER TABLE audit_events NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;</sql>
            <sql>ALTER TABLE audit_events DISABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="audit-1.0.0-030b-audit-events-rls-existing-partitions" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <!--
          Every partition that exists right now, discovered through pg_inherits rather than
          listed. 010b enumerated 13 partition names by hand and create_audit_partition() has
          been adding more since; a hardcoded list would silently stop covering the newest
          partition, which is the one every recent-events query reads and every ingest writes.
          Same reasoning as 013b, and the same shape.

          Guarded on pg_policies so that re-running against a partially-applied database is a
          no-op rather than a duplicate-object error.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            DECLARE
                part RECORD;
            BEGIN
                FOR part IN
                    SELECT c.relname
                    FROM pg_inherits i
                    JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = i.inhrelid
                    JOIN pg_class p ON p.oid = i.inhparent
                    WHERE p.relname = 'audit_events'
                LOOP
                    EXECUTE format('ALTER TABLE %I ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY', part.relname);
                    EXECUTE format('ALTER TABLE %I FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY', part.relname);
                    IF NOT EXISTS (
                        SELECT 1 FROM pg_policies
                        WHERE schemaname = 'public'
                          AND tablename = part.relname
                          AND policyname = 'tenant_isolation'
                    ) THEN
                        EXECUTE format(
                            'CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON %I USING (tenant_id = (NULLIF(current_setting(''app.current_tenant_id'', true), ''''))::uuid)',
                            part.relname
                        );
                    END IF;
                END LOOP;
            END $$;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="false">
                DO $$
                DECLARE
                    part RECORD;
                BEGIN
                    FOR part IN
                        SELECT c.relname
                        FROM pg_inherits i
                        JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = i.inhrelid
                        JOIN pg_class p ON p.oid = i.inhparent
                        WHERE p.relname = 'audit_events'
                    LOOP
                        EXECUTE format('DROP POLICY IF EXISTS tenant_isolation ON %I', part.relname);
                        EXECUTE format('ALTER TABLE %I NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY', part.relname);
                        EXECUTE format('ALTER TABLE %I DISABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY', part.relname);
                    END LOOP;
                END $$;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="audit-1.0.0-030c-future-partitions-protected" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <!--
          Future partitions. Without this the gap returns next month, on a date nobody is
          watching, and returns silently: a new partition would be created with no RLS at all,
          and every audit row written from that month onward would sit in a table any tenant
          could read directly. This is the single most important part of this changeset —
          measured above, a partition created after the parent is protected inherits NOTHING.

          Two changes to the function, on top of the 013c version it replaces (which granted
          INSERT and SELECT and is preserved verbatim below).

          1. It applies ENABLE + FORCE + the tenant_isolation policy to each partition it
             creates, immediately after creating it and inside the same statement batch, so
             there is no window in which the partition exists unprotected.

          2. It becomes SECURITY DEFINER, with search_path pinned. This is not incidental — it
             fixes a second, pre-existing defect that this changeset would otherwise trip over.
             AuditArchivalService calls create_audit_partition() on the RUNTIME datasource, i.e.
             as audit_writer, and measured live:

                 SELECT has_schema_privilege('audit_writer','public','CREATE');  ->  f

             audit_writer cannot create tables at all, so the monthly job's CREATE TABLE has been
             failing since PostgreSQL 15 stopped granting CREATE on public to PUBLIC. Partitions
             exist today only because 010b created them through Liquibase, and they run out after
             2027-01. CREATE POLICY and ALTER TABLE ... FORCE additionally require OWNERSHIP,
             which audit_writer would not have either. SECURITY DEFINER makes the function run as
             its owner — the role Liquibase runs as, which owns the parent and every existing
             partition — so partition creation, the grants and the RLS all succeed and every
             partition keeps a single consistent owner.

             search_path is pinned to 'public, pg_temp' because a SECURITY DEFINER function that
             inherits the caller's search_path lets the caller shadow the objects it references.
             pg_temp is placed last for the same reason.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION create_audit_partition(month_start DATE) RETURNS VOID AS $$
            DECLARE
                partition_name TEXT;
                month_end      DATE;
            BEGIN
                partition_name := 'audit_events_' || to_char(month_start, 'YYYY_MM');
                month_end      := month_start + INTERVAL '1 month';

                IF NOT EXISTS (
                    SELECT 1 FROM pg_class c
                    JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
                    WHERE c.relname = partition_name
                      AND n.nspname = 'public'
                ) THEN
                    EXECUTE format(
                        'CREATE TABLE %I PARTITION OF audit_events FOR VALUES FROM (%L) TO (%L)',
                        partition_name, month_start, month_end
                    );
                    -- INSERT and SELECT, and deliberately not UPDATE or DELETE (013c).
                    EXECUTE format('GRANT INSERT, SELECT ON %I TO audit_writer', partition_name);

                    -- Tenant isolation, applied to the partition itself. A policy on the parent
                    -- does not cover a query that names the partition directly.
                    EXECUTE format('ALTER TABLE %I ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY', partition_name);
                    EXECUTE format('ALTER TABLE %I FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY', partition_name);
                    EXECUTE format(
                        'CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON %I USING (tenant_id = (NULLIF(current_setting(''app.current_tenant_id'', true), ''''))::uuid)',
                        partition_name
                    );
                END IF;
            END;
            $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql
               SECURITY DEFINER
               SET search_path = public, pg_temp;
        </sql>

        <!--
          EXECUTE on a SECURITY DEFINER function defaults to PUBLIC, which would let any role in
          the database create partitions. Only the runtime user needs it.
        -->
        <sql>REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION create_audit_partition(DATE) FROM PUBLIC;</sql>
        <sql>GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION create_audit_partition(DATE) TO audit_writer;</sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql>SELECT 1;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
